.TH VAIL-CLI 1 "2026-11-03" "vail-cli 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
vail-cli \- decode every CW (Morse) signal a sound card can hear
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B vail-cli
.RI [ options ]
.RI [ file ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B vail-cli
decodes all CW signals between 200 and 3900 Hz at once, using the vail engine, and prints what they send
as lines of text. It listens to the system's default sound input unless told otherwise.
.PP
A
.I file
may be WAV, FLAC or MP3. A file of
.B \-
reads raw signed 16-bit little-endian samples, 8 kHz mono, from standard input.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-list
List the sound input devices and exit.
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-device " " \fIDEV\fR
Use this device: its number in the list, or any part of its name, in upper or lower case, that matches
only one device.
.TP
.BR \-b ", " \-\-band " " \fILO\-HI\fR
Only decode signals between
.I LO
and
.I HI
Hz.
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-record " " \fIFILE\fR
Also record the audio to a WAV file, at 8 kHz, for replaying later.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show the help and exit.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Show the version and exit.
.SH OUTPUT
Standard output carries one line for each finished piece of text:
.PP
.RS
.nf
15:33:16   500  CQ CQ CQ DE K1ABC K1ABC K1ABC PSE K
.fi
.RE
.PP
The fields are the time the text started, the pitch in Hz, and the text. The time is UTC for a sound device
and the time from the start for a file or a stream. Lower-case letters are prosigns.
.PP
A line is printed once its text is final: when the signal has been quiet for 4 seconds, or when 60 characters
can no longer be revised by the decoder. On a terminal, signals still being heard and the input level are
shown below the finished lines. They are never written to a pipe or a file.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success, including stopping with Ctrl+C.
.TP
.B 1
A runtime error, such as a missing device or a device that stops.
.TP
.B 2
A usage error.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
vail-cli -l
vail-cli -d "USB Audio"
vail-cli -d 2 -b 500-1500 -r session.wav
vail-cli | grep --line-buffered K1ABC
vail-cli recording.wav > text.log
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
https://github.com/TheRealKamisama/vail-decoder
